  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA quiet weather pattern is expected to persist through the\nweekend and much if not all of next week. \n\nA weak dry cold front passing through the forecast area today \nis producing some strong winds over northwest ND. A few hours of\nnear critical fire weather conditions are still forecast here \nthis afternoon. RAP forecast winds are right around their max\nover northwest ND at this time, and should start to diminish as\nthe stronger winds work eastward. \n\nAfter today we see an impulse tracking across the Rockies and \nthrough the forecast area Sunday night into Monday. Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday another clipper swings through, and drops \ninto a developing eastern U.S. Trough. This will bring some \ncooler temperatures to eastern portions of the state Wednesday\nand Thursday, while the west remains mild. Highs will likely\nrange from the lower 40s east to the lower 60s west on Thursday.\nStrong ridging then builds over the central portion of the \ncountry late in the workweek and remains through the weekend, \nwith troughs over the eastern U.S. and the eastern Pacific. \nThere may be a brief shower with each wave, but most will likely\nremain dry. Currently the NBM does not contain PoPs through the\nentire forecast period. Mainly we temperature swings and wind \nshifts as each system passes by.",
